// STRUDELTUTOR 01/22 | Welcome: edit, evaluate, listen
//
// Welcome to Strudeltutor! Learn by changing this save.
// Allow 30-45 minutes; take each lesson at your own pace.
// This is original tutorial text, inspired by vimtutor.
//
// GET READY
// 1. /tutor opens a working copy in your config directory.
//    Ctrl+S, :w or F2 /save saves that copy.
//    Opened this JSON directly? First save your own copy:
//    F2 /save ~/strudeltutor-work.json Enter.
// 2. F2 /song off Enter keeps lessons from auto-advancing.
// 3. F9 shows lessons. Ctrl+N next; Ctrl+B previous.
// 4. F1 shows keys. Close F1 before paging the editor.
//    Arrows / PgUp / PgDn move through this buffer.
// 5. F3 toggles vi mode. In vi normal mode, i starts typing;
//    Escape returns to normal. Ordinary editing works too.
//
// Each lesson is a separate, runnable song track.
// F5 evaluates your edits AND commits this lesson's buffer.
// Press F5 before changing lessons to retain your work.
// Ctrl+S saves the whole tutorial, including other lessons.
// Ctrl+Z undoes an edit; F5 makes the undo audible.
// Answers are comments below each playground. Try first!
//
// DO IT
// The starter is silent on purpose. Change gain(0) to
// gain(0.5), then F5. If transport is stopped, Ctrl+P plays.
// Ctrl+P also stops the sound. F5 evaluates; it does not save
// your file. Aim for a comfortable listening level.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4").s('sine').gain(0)

// HEAR: One clear C repeats. Change c4 to g4; F5: higher!
// DONE: You can edit, apply, stop and save your work.
// F5, then Ctrl+N for lesson 02. Keep song mode off.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("g4").s('sine').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 02/22 | Your first note pattern
//
// note(...) chooses pitches; s(...) chooses the instrument.
// A dot chains a transformation onto a pattern.
// Either quote style can contain mini-notation. Here the
// single-quoted string names an instrument. Keep both pairs.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change "c4" to "c4 e4 g4 c5". F5.
// 2. Change sine to triangle. F5. Compare the tone.
// 3. Replace c5 with b4; F5. Listen, undo, then F5 again.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4").s('sine').gain(0.5)

// HEAR: Four equally spaced notes repeat after step 1.
// CHECK: More notes share the cycle; it does not get longer.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 03/22 | Cycles and tempo
//
// A cycle is the repeating time container, not a note.
// These lessons use four beats per cycle: 108 BPM / 4
// gives 27 cycles per minute, about 2.22 seconds per cycle.
// setcpm(...) sets cycles per minute for this buffer.
//
// DO IT
// 1. In setcpm above the playground, replace 108 with 72.
//    F5. The WHOLE pattern slows down.
// 2. Restore 108. Add four more notes inside the quotes.
//    F5. Twice as many notes now fit the SAME cycle.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 d4 e4 g4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// HEAR: Tempo changes cycle length; adding steps changes
// note density within that cycle. Next lessons reset tempo.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 d4 e4 g4 c5 g4 e4 d4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 04/22 | Rests make rhythm
//
// ~ is a rest: it occupies a step without playing a note.
// A space separates steps in the same cycle.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Replace the second c4 with ~. F5.
// 2. Replace the last c4 with ~ too. F5.
// 3. Try "c4 ~ ~ c4". Clap along: where is the gap?

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 c4 c4 c4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// HEAR: "c4 ~ c4 ~" has two notes in four time slots.
// CHECK: A rest makes room; deleting the slot changes timing.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 ~ c4 ~").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 05/22 | Subdivide with brackets
//
// [ ... ] fits its contents into ONE outer time slot.
// The pattern below has four outer slots. The second slot
// holds two notes, so those notes are closer together.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Replace [d4 e4] with [d4 e4 f4]. F5.
// 2. Replace g4 with [g4 ~ g4]. F5.
// 3. Try [d4 [e4 f4]] in the second slot. F5.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 [d4 e4] g4 ~").s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// HEAR: Inner groups get busier without moving outer slots.
// CHECK: Brackets nest. Every opening [ needs a closing ].
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 [d4 [e4 f4]] [g4 ~ g4] ~").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 06/22 | Repeat: * versus !
//
// *2 subdivides an item's existing slot into two repeats.
// !2 expands an item into two copies at its own level.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Listen to "c4*2 e4": two quick Cs, then a longer E.
// 2. Change * to !; F5. Hear THREE equally spaced notes.
// 3. Try "[c4 e4]*2 g4". F5. The repeated group shares
//    the first half; g4 keeps the second half.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4*2 e4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// CHECK: "c4!2 e4" is timed like "c4 c4 e4".
// HEAR: * squeezes repeats in; ! adds sibling steps.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("[c4 e4]*2 g4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 07/22 | Alternate across cycles
//
// < ... > chooses one item per cycle, in order.
// Square brackets divide time WITHIN a cycle; angle
// brackets choose material ACROSS successive cycles.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Listen for at least four cycles. The first note changes.
// 2. Add e4 inside <c4 g4>. F5; listen for three cycles.
// 3. Replace the angles with [c4 g4 e4]. F5. Compare.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("<c4 g4> e4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// CHECK: <c4 g4 e4> e4 starts on C, G, E over three cycles.
// HEAR: [c4 g4 e4] e4 plays all three in every cycle.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("<c4 g4 e4> e4").s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 08/22 | Play a chord with commas
//
// Commas stack patterns at the same time.
// "c4,e4,g4" is three simultaneous notes, not a sequence.
// Keep gain modest when adding simultaneous voices.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Replace the spaces in "c4 e4 g4" with commas. F5.
// 2. Lower e4 to eb4. F5. Major becomes minor.
// 3. Try "[c4,eb4,g4] [f4,ab4,c5]" for two chords.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 e4 g4").s('triangle').gain(0.35)

// HEAR: All notes in each chord start together.
// CHECK: # raises a pitch; b lowers it, e.g. f#4 and eb4.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("[c4,eb4,g4] [f4,ab4,c5]").s('triangle').gain(0.35)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 09/22 | Euclidean rhythms
//
// (pulses,steps) distributes pulses over a step grid.
// c3(3,8) plays C three times across eight steps per cycle.
// An optional third number rotates the rhythm.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Try c3(5,8), then c3(3,8,1). F5 each time.
// 2. Change 8 to 16, keeping 3 pulses. Listen to the gaps.
// 3. Build a second half with "c3(3,8) g3(5,8)".

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c3(3,8)").s('triangle').decay(0.12).sustain(0).gain(0.6)

// HEAR: Changing pulses changes density; rotation shifts
// where the rhythm starts without adding pulses.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c3(3,8) g3(5,8)").s('triangle').decay(0.12).sustain(0).gain(0.6)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 10/22 | Transform time with fast and slow
//
// .fast(2) plays the whole pattern twice as quickly.
// .slow(2) stretches the whole pattern over two cycles.
// Unlike changing setcpm, a transform affects its branch.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change fast(1) to fast(2). F5.
// 2. Replace fast(2) with slow(2). F5; listen longer.
// 3. Try fast(2).slow(2). Predict the result first.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').fast(1).gain(0.5)

// CHECK: fast(2).slow(2) returns to the original timing.
// HEAR: The pitches stay the same as time changes.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').slow(2).gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 11/22 | Scale degrees, arithmetic and transposition
//
// n(...) supplies numbers. With scale('C:minor'), they
// become scale degrees: 0 is the root, 1 the next degree.
// note(...) directly specifies pitches / MIDI note numbers.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change minor to major. F5. Hear the third brighten.
// 2. Change C to D. F5. The whole scale moves up.
// 3. Restore C:minor, append .transpose(7) AFTER .scale(...).
//    F5. transpose shifts resolved notes by semitones.
// 4. Switch to C:major and remove transpose(7). Replace
//    n(...) with n("0 2 4".add("<0 3 4 0>")). F5.
//    Listen for four cycles: C-E-G, F-A-C, G-B-D, C-E-G.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
n("0 2 4 6").scale('C:minor').s('triangle').gain(0.5)

// CHECK: Change the numbers inside n(...) to shift DEGREES.
// add INSIDE n(...) shifts raw numbers before scale maps
// them to pitches. add(3) means three scale degrees here.
// Use transpose(...) after scale(...) to shift semitones.
// Bare n(...).add(7) is control-pattern arithmetic: avoid it.
//
// BONUS: Arithmetic also works on raw note-name patterns:
// note("c3 e3 g3".add("<0 5 7 0>")).s('triangle').gain(0.5)
// Note names become MIDI numbers; these offsets are semitones.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | n("0 2 4".add("<0 3 4 0>")).scale('C:major')
// |   .s('triangle').gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 12/22 | Patterns can control anything
//
// A control can take a pattern, not just a single number.
// Here gain alternates between strong and soft steps.
// Each control string spans its own cycle. Four values
// align with these four notes; two values span half each.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change gain's string to "0.6 0 0.25 0". F5.
// 2. Restore "0.6 0.15 0.6 0.15"; add .pan("0 1 0 1"). F5.
//    Pan 0 is left; 1 is right. Listen through stereo output.
// 3. Try gain("<0.6 0.2>") for one level per cycle.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').gain("0.6 0.15 0.6 0.15")

// HEAR: A zero gain mutes a step; pan changes its position.
// CHECK: Mini-notation also works in gain, pan and filters.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').gain("0.6 0.15 0.6 0.15").pan("0 1 0 1")
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 13/22 | Layer parts with named labels
//
// Named labels collect concurrent patterns in one buffer.
// melody and bass below both play, each with its own sound.
// A leading or trailing underscore mutes a named label.
// $: also plays a branch; labels help you identify parts.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Rename bass: to bass_: and F5. It is muted.
// 2. Restore bass:. Change bass's slow(2) to slow(4).
// 3. Change only melody's pitches. F5. Bass stays intact.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
melody: note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle').gain(0.45)
bass: note("c2 g2").s('sine').slow(2).gain(0.6)

// HEAR: Two independently timed parts play together.
// CHECK: Muting a label removes that part, not the others.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | melody: note("c4 d4 eb4 g4").s('triangle').gain(0.45)
// | bass: note("c2 g2").s('sine').slow(4).gain(0.6)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 14/22 | Shape notes with an envelope
//
// ADSR = attack, decay, sustain level, release.
// attack/decay/release use seconds; sustain is a level.
// A short decay and zero sustain make a pluck.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change decay(0.15) to decay(0.5). F5.
// 2. Try attack(0.2), sustain(0.4), release(0.3).
// 3. Restore a short attack and sustain(0). Compare.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle')
  .attack(0.005).decay(0.15).sustain(0).release(0.05)
  .gain(0.6)

// HEAR: The note's contour changes without changing pitch.
// CHECK: Decay is a time; sustain is a level, not a time.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 e4 g4 c5").s('triangle')
// |   .attack(0.2).decay(0.5).sustain(0.4).release(0.3)
// |   .gain(0.6)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 15/22 | Hear a low-pass filter
//
// Sawtooth has bright harmonics. lpf(...) lets frequencies
// below its cutoff through and softens higher frequencies.
// The cutoff is in hertz, not a note number.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change lpf(400) to lpf(4000). F5.
// 2. Try lpf("400 1200 4000 1200"). F5.
// 3. Change sawtooth to sine; compare the filter's effect.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c3 e3 g3 c4").s('sawtooth').lpf(400).gain(0.4)

// HEAR: A higher cutoff brightens the sawtooth.
// CHECK: A sine has fewer harmonics for the filter to remove.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c3 e3 g3 c4").s('sawtooth').lpf("400 1200 4000 1200").gain(0.4)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 16/22 | Move controls with a signal
//
// sine is also a continuous control signal (without quotes).
// sine.range(300,3000) maps its motion to filter cutoff.
// .slow(4) makes that control complete a turn in four cycles.
// The control value is sampled at each scheduled event.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Replace slow(4) inside lpf with slow(1). F5.
// 2. Try range(800,5000). F5. Compare brightness.
// 3. Add .pan(sine.range(0.2,0.8).slow(2)). F5.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c3*8").s('sawtooth')
  .lpf(sine.range(300,3000).slow(4)).gain(0.4)

// HEAR: Successive notes open and close the filter.
// CHECK: slow on the signal changes modulation, not notes.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c3*8").s('sawtooth')
// |   .lpf(sine.range(800,5000).slow(1))
// |   .pan(sine.range(0.2,0.8).slow(2)).gain(0.4)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 17/22 | Repeat with a change
//
// .every(4, x => x.rev()) reverses cycles 0, 4, 8, ...
// The change starts on the FIRST cycle of each group of four.
// x => ... is a function that transforms the given pattern.
// .sometimes(...) applies a change probabilistically.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Listen for at least five cycles, then change 4 to 2. F5.
//    every(2, ...) changes cycles 0, 2, 4, ... instead.
// 2. Change x.rev() to x.fast(2). F5.
// 3. Try .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)) instead of every(...).
//    ply repeats events inside their existing durations.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 d4 e4 g4").s('triangle')
  .every(4, x => x.rev()).gain(0.5)

// HEAR: A recurring variation breaks the plain loop.
// CHECK: every is periodic; sometimes need not repeat exactly.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 d4 e4 g4").s('triangle')
// |   .every(2, x => x.fast(2)).gain(0.5)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 18/22 | Reuse a musical idea with JavaScript
//
// const names a reusable value. A function can turn a
// pattern into an instrument part. stack(...) plays parts
// together. Pattern transforms return new patterns.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change just motif's notes; F5. Both parts change.
// 2. Change the second part's transpose(12) to transpose(7). F5.
// 3. Make a pluck function as in the hint, then use it.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
const motif = note("c4 e4 g4 e4")
stack(
  motif.s('triangle').gain(0.45),
  motif.transpose(12).slow(2).s('sine').gain(0.3)
)

// HEAR: One idea produces two related voices.
// CHECK: transpose(12) transposes up an octave; slow(2) keeps
// the second part at half speed.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | const motif = note("c4 d4 eb4 g4")
// | const pluck = p => p.s('triangle').decay(0.15).sustain(0)
// | stack(
// |   pluck(motif).gain(0.45),
// |   pluck(motif.transpose(7).slow(2)).gain(0.3)
// | )
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 19/22 | Percussion and the sound browser
//
// Make percussion from built-in synths: a short low sine
// for a pulse, and filtered noise for a hat. No bank needed.
// s('white') is noise; here note provides event timing.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change pulse's c2*4 to "c2 ~ c2 [~ c2]". F5.
// 2. Change hat's c6*8 to "c6*8?0.3". F5.
//    ?0.3 drops each event with probability 0.3.
// 3. OPTIONAL: F6, find bd or hh, Enter inserts a sound
//    snippet at your cursor. Use a new line for a new part.
//    Sample availability depends on your installed banks.
//    Try s("bd sd, hh*8").gain(0.5) as an alternative.
//    For a whole extra branch, put $: before that pattern.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
pulse: note("c2*4").s('sine')
  .decay(0.1).sustain(0).gain(0.75)
hat: note("c6*8").s('white').hpf(6000)
  .decay(0.025).sustain(0).gain(0.15)

// HEAR: Low regular pulses and light high-frequency ticks.
// CHECK: The starter works offline without downloaded samples.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | pulse: note("c2 ~ c2 [~ c2]").s('sine')
// |   .decay(0.1).sustain(0).gain(0.75)
// | hat: note("c6*8?0.3").s('white').hpf(6000)
// |   .decay(0.025).sustain(0).gain(0.15)
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 20/22 | Space, sliders and visual feedback
//
// room adds reverb; delay sends notes to an echo.
// Start with small sends so the original notes stay clear.
// _pianoroll shows notes; _scope shows this branch's audio.
// Visual calls can attach immediately; musical edits need F5.
//
// DO IT
// 1. Change room(0) to room(0.2); add .delay(0.15). F5.
// 2. Replace lpf(1000) with lpf(slider(1000,200,4000,100)).
//    F5. Ctrl+Left/Right adjusts and evaluates the slider.
// 3. Add ._scope() after ._pianoroll(). Watch the waveform.
// 4. In .gain(0.4), remove 'in' to make .ga(0.4).
//    Place the cursor just after ga; Tab restores gain. F5.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
note("c4 ~ e4 g4").s('sawtooth').lpf(1000)
  .decay(0.18).sustain(0).room(0).gain(0.4)
  ._pianoroll()

// HEAR: The gaps fill with short echoes / reverb tails.
// CHECK: Each visual follows its attached pattern branch.
//
// HINT / ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER - comments, not extra voices.
// | note("c4 ~ e4 g4").s('sawtooth')
// |   .lpf(slider(1000,200,4000,100))
// |   .decay(0.18).sustain(0).room(0.2).delay(0.15)
// |   .gain(0.4)._pianoroll()._scope()
//
// F5 commits this lesson; Ctrl+N next, Ctrl+B previous.
// STRUDELTUTOR 21/22 | Recover from a mistake
//
// Mistakes are part of live coding. Read the console first.
// On a failed F5, Tompouce retains the last playable pattern.
// Edits are not audible until a successful evaluation.
//
// DO IT
// 1. F5 the valid starter once. Remove its final closing ).
//    F5. Find the evaluation error in the console.
// 2. Undo with Ctrl+Z (vi normal: u). F5 to recover.
// 3. Replace g4 with [g4 INSIDE the string. F5. This is
//    a mini-notation error, rather than a missing JS bracket.
//    Restore g4; F5. Fix one small change at a time.
// 4. F4 shows state; close it again. F1 opens keybindings.

setcpm(108 / 4)

// PLAYGROUND - edit the executable code below.
